From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Xun2202 <98207886+Xun2202@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 04:44:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] database: busy timeout and Room-like connection setup to
 avoid "database is locked"
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Crash seen on HarmonyOS/卓易通 (Android 16 compat layer) right after relaunching
from the crash screen:

    android.database.SQLException: Error code: 5, message: database is locked
        at androidx.sqlite.driver.bundled.BundledSQLiteStatementKt.nativeStep
        at com.eygraber.sqldelight.androidx.driver.AndroidxPreparedStatement.execute
        at com.eygraber.sqldelight.androidx.driver.AndroidxSqliteExecutingDriver.executeStatement

SQLITE_BUSY surfaces as a fatal exception because v0.20.4 opens tachiyomi.db
without a busy timeout: the first time a write statement meets a lock held by
another connection it fails immediately instead of waiting. Locks from
outside the single writer pool do occur:

- CrashActivity runs in the ":error_handler" process, and App.onCreate()
  (WidgetManager, migrator) opens the same database there. When the user taps
  "restart" the main process starts while the crash process is still alive.
- After the process is killed (e.g. the dataSync foreground service timeout
  on Android 15+), the next open has to recover the WAL file, during which
  other connections get SQLITE_BUSY.

Backport of upstream mihonapp/mihon@38e93086c8 ("Configure database
connections like Room"), adapted to the Injekt AppModule of v0.20.4:

- every connection (writer and readers) runs `PRAGMA busy_timeout = 3000`
  via a custom AndroidxSqliteConnectionFactory, so a short lock is waited
  out instead of failing;
- journal mode is set explicitly: WAL unless the device is low on RAM
  (TRUNCATE there), and the pool is one writer + four readers (one reader in
  TRUNCATE mode) via MultipleReadersSingleWriter(nonWalCount = 1, walCount = 4).

Upstream's `isWal = isLowRamDevice ?: true` reads inverted relative to its
own comment ("WAL unless the device is low on RAM"); this patch follows the
documented intent, which also keeps existing databases in the WAL mode the
driver already used, so no journal-mode switch happens on upgrade.

The sqldelight-androidx-driver build pinned by v0.20.4 (0637a964a1) already
provides AndroidxSqliteConnectionFactory, SqliteJournalMode and the
connectionFactory constructor; no dependency bump is needed.
---
 .../java/eu/kanade/tachiyomi/di/AppModule.kt  | 25 ++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 24 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/app/src/main/java/eu/kanade/tachiyomi/di/AppModule.kt b/app/src/main/java/eu/kanade/tachiyomi/di/AppModule.kt
index ed3f940..e0bfdf1 100644
--- a/app/src/main/java/eu/kanade/tachiyomi/di/AppModule.kt
+++ b/app/src/main/java/eu/kanade/tachiyomi/di/AppModule.kt
@@ -1,14 +1,22 @@
 package eu.kanade.tachiyomi.di
 
+import android.app.ActivityManager
 import android.app.Application
 import android.content.Context
 import androidx.core.content.ContextCompat
+import androidx.core.content.getSystemService
+import androidx.sqlite.SQLiteConnection
+import androidx.sqlite.SQLiteDriver
 import androidx.sqlite.driver.bundled.BundledSQLiteDriver
+import androidx.sqlite.execSQL
 import app.cash.sqldelight.db.SqlDriver
+import com.eygraber.sqldelight.androidx.driver.AndroidxSqliteConcurrencyModel.MultipleReadersSingleWriter
 import com.eygraber.sqldelight.androidx.driver.AndroidxSqliteConfiguration
+import com.eygraber.sqldelight.androidx.driver.AndroidxSqliteConnectionFactory
 import com.eygraber.sqldelight.androidx.driver.AndroidxSqliteDatabaseType
 import com.eygraber.sqldelight.androidx.driver.AndroidxSqliteDriver
 import com.eygraber.sqldelight.androidx.driver.FileProvider
+import com.eygraber.sqldelight.androidx.driver.SqliteJournalMode
 import eu.kanade.domain.track.store.DelayedTrackingStore
 import eu.kanade.tachiyomi.data.cache.ChapterCache
 import eu.kanade.tachiyomi.data.cache.CoverCache
@@ -60,12 +68,27 @@ class AppModule(val app: Application) : InjektModule {
             synchronized(lock) {
                 sqlDriverRef?.get()?.let { return@synchronized it }
 
+                // Configured like Room's connection manager: WAL unless the device is low on RAM, one writer
+                // with four readers (one in TRUNCATE mode), and a busy timeout on every connection so that a
+                // lock held by another connection or process (e.g. the :error_handler process, or WAL recovery
+                // after the app was killed) is waited out instead of failing with "database is locked".
+                val isWal = app.getSystemService<ActivityManager>()?.isLowRamDevice != true
                 AndroidxSqliteDriver(
-                    driver = BundledSQLiteDriver(),
+                    connectionFactory = object : AndroidxSqliteConnectionFactory {
+                        override val driver: SQLiteDriver = BundledSQLiteDriver()
+
+                        override fun createConnection(name: String): SQLiteConnection {
+                            return driver.open(name).apply {
+                                execSQL("PRAGMA busy_timeout = 3000")
+                            }
+                        }
+                    },
                     databaseType = AndroidxSqliteDatabaseType.FileProvider(app, "tachiyomi.db"),
                     schema = Database.Schema,
                     configuration = AndroidxSqliteConfiguration(
                         isForeignKeyConstraintsEnabled = true,
+                        journalMode = if (isWal) SqliteJournalMode.WAL else SqliteJournalMode.Truncate,
+                        concurrencyModel = MultipleReadersSingleWriter(isWal = isWal, nonWalCount = 1, walCount = 4),
                     ),
                 )
                     .also { sqlDriverRef = WeakReference(it) }
